# Refract — Controls

This file defines how a player operates the game: the pointer draws beams on the
board and works every screen's controls, and the keyboard does the same from the
other side. Which segments the rules permit is in `specs/beams.md`, and the cell
centers the pointer is measured against are in `specs/board.md`. Every figure
below carries the name this specification gives it.

## The pointer

A mouse, a pen, and a finger on a touchscreen all drive the pointer, and the
game reads them the same way. Everything the pointer does below is done by all
three.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
This game stands on no engine, so the pointer belongs to the runtime layer you
write. What the game reads from that layer is a pointer position in the game's
logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, a
release edge, and which of `mouse`, `pen`, and `touch` drove it. That layer
delivers all three devices on that one path, and it takes the browser's own
gestures on the canvas — panning, pinch-zoom, double-tap zoom, text selection,
and the context menu — so a drag on a touchscreen reaches the game whole rather
than being taken for a scroll part way through. How the layer produces those is
yours to decide.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The pointer belongs to the engine, which documents it in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it is a
pointer position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, a release edge, and the device that drove
it.
{{else}}
The pointer belongs to the engine, which documents it in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it is a
pointer position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, a release edge, and the device that drove
it, delivered as the ordered samples the player controller reads from the engine
each frame, so a sweep that crossed several nodes between two frames arrives as
every position it visited rather than as the last one alone.
{{/if}}

The game acts on the primary pointer alone, so a second finger resting on the
screen changes nothing. Each pointer position the game reads is resolved on its
own, in the order the positions arrive, so a beam grows and unwinds one node at a
time as the pointer travels.

`state.pointer` mirrors the position, the press state, and the device every
frame, as `specs/state.md` describes.

## Pointer targets

Every screen carries a set of pointer targets: axis-aligned rectangles the
pointer operates the screen through. A target has an id, a position, and a size
in the stage's logical units, and `specs/instrumentation.md` reports the current
screen's targets under those ids.

| Screen | Targets, in this order |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | `menu-0`, `menu-1`, `menu-2`, one per entry of `TITLE_ITEMS` |
| `howto` | `back` |
| `select` | `board-1` through `board-24`, then `back` |
| `playing` | `clear`, `back` |
| `solved` | one `menu-<i>` per choice the screen offers, in the order it offers them |
| `complete` | `menu-0`, `menu-1` |

Every target on every screen satisfies all four of these:

| Requirement | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Width | at least `TARGET_MIN_W` (`96`) |
| Height | at least `TARGET_MIN_H` (`72`) |
| Placement | wholly within the stage |
| Separation | no two targets on one screen overlap |

The width and the height are what a fingertip needs, so every target is worked by
touch as readily as by a mouse.

A `menu-<i>` target covers the drawn menu item that `state.menuIndex` `i`
highlights, and a `board-<n>` target covers the drawn grid cell for campaign
board `n`. The `back` target covers a drawn control reading `BACK_LABEL` (`BACK`)
and the `clear` target one reading `CLEAR_LABEL` (`CLEAR`), each legible at the
stage size. What each target looks like is the build's to design, within the
requirements in `specs/overview.md`.

On `playing` the `clear` and `back` targets sit clear of the board, whose extent
is in `specs/board.md`, so a target never covers a node.

## Operating a screen with the pointer

A pointer position lies in at most one target, since no two on a screen overlap.

| The pointer | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| Moves within a `menu-<i>` target while pressed on nothing | `state.menuIndex` becomes `i`. |
| Moves within a `board-<n>` target while pressed on nothing | `state.selectIndex` becomes `n - 1`. |
| Presses within a target | The highlight moves as the rows above describe, and that target is armed. |
| Releases within the armed target | The target is taken, and it is disarmed. |
| Releases anywhere else | Nothing is taken, and the armed target is disarmed. |

Taking a target does exactly what the same choice does from the keyboard:

| Target | Taken |
| --- | --- |
| `menu-<i>` | The same as `confirm` with `state.menuIndex` at `i`. |
| `board-<n>` | The same as `confirm` on `select` with `state.selectIndex` at `n - 1`. |
| `back` | The same as the `back` action on that screen. |
| `clear` | The same as the `clear` action. |

A press outside every target arms nothing. Leaving a screen disarms whatever was
armed on it, so a press carried across a screen change takes nothing.

The highlight moving on a press is what makes a touchscreen behave as a mouse
does: a finger has no hover, so the press is what tells the player which item
their release will take.

## Targeting a node

The pointer targets the node whose cell center lies within `NODE_HIT_R` (`44`) of
the pointer position. No two targeting regions overlap, so at most one node is
ever targeted. The pointer targets no node when it is farther than `NODE_HIT_R`
from every cell center, and an empty cell holds no node to target.

## Beginning a trace

A trace runs from a press to the matching release. A press that targets a node
begins one according to that node's place on the board. The rows below are
**evaluated in order**, and the first row that matches is the one that applies.

| Targeted node | What the press does |
| --- | --- |
| An emitter of a channel whose beam carries no segments | Starts that channel's beam at that emitter. |
| Either end of a channel's beam | Resumes that beam from that end. |
| A node exactly one beam passes through | Shortens that beam to end at that node, and resumes it from there. |
| Anything else | Begins no trace and leaves the board unchanged. |

A press inside the `clear` or the `back` target is taken by that target and begins
no trace, whatever lies behind it, so the two are resolved before the board is.

A node reaches the last row only once every row above it has been tried and
missed, so a node two beams run through reaches it only when it is **neither
beam's end**. The last row also catches a press on an empty cell, a press off
the board, and a press on a node that carries no beam and is not an emitter of a
channel whose beam carries no segments.

The trace carries the channel of the beam it began, and the node it began at is
the live end.

## The drawn order and shortening

A beam's cells are held in the order they were drawn, starting from the node the
trace that created the beam began at. Whenever a trace begins on a beam, the beam
is first oriented so that the node the trace extends from is its **last** cell,
reversing the held order when the trace begins from the other end. The live end
is therefore always the beam's last cell.

Shortening drops every cell after the targeted node in that order, leaves the
rest of the beam drawn, and resumes the trace from the targeted node. A beam that
crosses a node more than once carries that node more than once, and the last of
those occurrences in the held order is the one kept, so the beam loses as few
segments as it can.

## Extending

While the trace is held, the pointer moving within `NODE_HIT_R` of a node
adjacent to the live end adds the segment joining them, when the rules in
`specs/beams.md` permit that segment. That node becomes the new live end.

A move those rules refuse changes nothing and the trace stays live, so a player
can sweep the pointer across the board and draw only permitted segments.

## Retracting

The pointer moving within `NODE_HIT_R` of the node immediately behind the live
end removes the last segment, and that node becomes the live end. A beam is
unwound one segment at a time by backing the pointer along it without releasing.

## Releasing

The release edge ends the trace and leaves the beam exactly as drawn, complete or
not. A partial beam persists on the board, and a later press on either of its
ends resumes it. A beam persists only once it carries a segment. A trace
released at the node it began on added none, and the release takes back the one
cell the press placed: that channel's beam is left carrying **no cells at all**,
so a later press on either of that channel's emitters starts the beam afresh.
The single cell a beam holds between its press and its release lives only while
that trace is live, and it is the one thing a release does not leave as drawn.

A trace also ends the moment the board is solved. On the frame a segment
satisfies the solved condition the trace ends and the beams stay exactly as
drawn, and the release edge that follows begins nothing and changes nothing.

## Clearing

The `clear` action empties every beam on the board at once and leaves the board's
nodes untouched, so a player can start a board over without leaving it. It is
read on the `playing` screen and does nothing on any other screen. Taking the
`clear` target does the same thing.

## Actions and bindings

`ACTIONS` holds the actions the game registers with the runtime, and `BINDINGS`
maps each action to the keys that fire it. Each action is read once per frame as
a press edge.

| Action | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | Moves the menu highlight up. |
| `down` | Moves the menu highlight down. |
| `left` | Moves the menu highlight left. |
| `right` | Moves the menu highlight right. |
| `confirm` | Accepts the highlighted item. |
| `back` | Leaves the current screen. |
| `clear` | Empties every beam on the board. |
| `mute` | Toggles sound, from any screen. |

`clear` is bound to `KeyR`. The remaining bindings are yours, and each action
carries the keys a player reaches for by habit. The screens these actions move
between, and where each one leads, are in `specs/ui.md`,
`specs/modes/campaign.md`, and `specs/modes/cascade.md`.

Every screen is reachable and every choice on it is takeable from the pointer
alone and from the keyboard alone, so the game is played end to end with either.
